{
  "campaign_id": "doune-castle-tour",
  "content": {
    "dialogues": [
      {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "dlg/approach",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Who was he?",
                "next": "dlg/approach-albany"
              },
              {
                "label": "Why three ditches?",
                "next": "dlg/approach-ditches"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-approach",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Stand a moment and look at it before we go in. Robert Stewart put this up in the 1380s, where the Ardoch runs into the Teith, and the road north went past his front door — which was the point. Three ditches and a bank on this side. The other three sides the ground does for him."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/approach-albany",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Why three ditches?",
                "next": "dlg/approach-ditches"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/approach"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-approach",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Duke of Albany. The king's brother, and for the best part of thirty years the man who actually ran Scotland. He sealed a charter in there in 1381, so the walls were up by then and he was using them."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/approach-ditches",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Who was he?",
                "next": "dlg/approach-albany"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/approach"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-approach",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Because this is the only flat way in. Anywhere else you are coming up a bank with somebody above you. Go down into one if you like — they are dry, and they are cut in steps, so you will get out again."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/gate-passage",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "What is a yett?",
                "next": "dlg/gate-yett"
              },
              {
                "label": "Who held this?",
                "next": "dlg/gate-garrison"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-gate-passage",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Look up before you walk through. Portcullis in its slot, the iron yett folded back against the wall there, and those holes in the vault are not for light. Twenty paces of that, with a guardroom on each side of you."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/gate-yett",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Who held this?",
                "next": "dlg/gate-garrison"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/gate-passage"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-gate-passage",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "A lattice gate of iron bars, forged so the bars pass through one another. You cannot cut a bar out of it without cutting four. Scotland kept making them long after everyone else had given up."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/gate-garrison",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "What is a yett?",
                "next": "dlg/gate-yett"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/gate-passage"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-gate-passage",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "A porter who slept by the door, and whatever garrison the duke was paying that year. The room on your right was theirs. The pit under the floor on the left was for people the duke was not paying."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/courtyard",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Was it besieged?",
                "next": "dlg/courtyard-siege"
              },
              {
                "label": "Who lived here?",
                "next": "dlg/courtyard-household"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-courtyard",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Four ranges were planned. Three were built. That gap on the south side has been a gap for six hundred years. The well is eighteen metres down, and a well is what a siege actually turns on — not the walls."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/courtyard-siege",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Who lived here?",
                "next": "dlg/courtyard-household"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/courtyard"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-courtyard",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Rarely, and never for long. The famous night here is 1746: the Jacobites held the place and kept prisoners upstairs, and some of them went out of a window on knotted bedsheets. One of them wrote a play about it afterwards."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/courtyard-household",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Was it besieged?",
                "next": "dlg/courtyard-siege"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/courtyard"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-courtyard",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Forty or so, when the duke was in residence, and half a dozen when he was not. Everyone crossed this yard twenty times a day — food one way, water the other, and the garrison walking over your head the whole time."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/lords-hall",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Is this original?",
                "next": "dlg/lords-original"
              },
              {
                "label": "Why two fires?",
                "next": "dlg/lords-fireplace"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-lords-hall",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "The duke's own hall — and mind where it is. Directly over the gate. He sat above the door he controlled, with his own stair and his own fire, and this tower could hold out after the rest of the castle had gone."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/lords-original",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Why two fires?",
                "next": "dlg/lords-fireplace"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/lords-hall"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-lords-hall",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "The walls are his. The gallery and the panelling are 1883 — an earl put the roofs back on, which is the only reason you are standing in a room instead of in a ruin with grass on it."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/lords-fireplace",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Is this original?",
                "next": "dlg/lords-original"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/lords-hall"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-lords-hall",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "One hearth this size would smoke you out. Two smaller ones side by side heat the room and let you stand between them, which is what the duke did while other people stood in the draught and asked him for things."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/duchess-hall",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "What is the basin?",
                "next": "dlg/duchess-piscina"
              },
              {
                "label": "Whose widow?",
                "next": "dlg/duchess-queens"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-duchess-hall",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Her hall, one floor up, and her oratory in the thickness of the wall there: altar, a basin for the vessels, a shelf for the bread. In 1425 the king executed the duke's son at Stirling and took Doune for the crown. After that it was a hunting lodge and a widow's house."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/duchess-piscina",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Whose widow?",
                "next": "dlg/duchess-queens"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/duchess-hall"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-duchess-hall",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "A piscina. The water used at the altar is not poured away like washing-up water — it goes down that drain and into the ground of the castle itself. The niche beside it held the bread and wine before the priest needed them."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/duchess-queens",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "What is the basin?",
                "next": "dlg/duchess-piscina"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/duchess-hall"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-duchess-hall",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Three of them, one after another. A dower house is where a queen goes when her husband dies and her son's wife wants her palace. They hunted, they held court in the room below, and they left the fabric alone, which is why so much of it is here."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/great-hall",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "No chimney?",
                "next": "dlg/hall-louvre"
              },
              {
                "label": "Who came here?",
                "next": "dlg/hall-montrose"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-great-hall",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "High table on the dais, everybody else down the length of the room in order of how much the duke liked them, and the fire in the middle of the floor with a hole in the ridge over it. No chimney anywhere in this room."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/hall-louvre",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Who came here?",
                "next": "dlg/hall-montrose"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/great-hall"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-great-hall",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "The smoke goes up and out of the louvre, and on the way it seasons the rafters, the ham and everybody's clothes. People smelled of their own hall. You would know a household by the smell of its servants."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/hall-montrose",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "No chimney?",
                "next": "dlg/hall-louvre"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/great-hall"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-great-hall",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Montrose quartered here in 1645, in the middle of the wars, and the hall fed his officers. It is one of the best preserved halls in Scotland, and the reason is dull: nobody rich enough to modernise it ever wanted to live here."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/kitchen",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "What is the oven?",
                "next": "dlg/kitchen-oven"
              },
              {
                "label": "How many cooked?",
                "next": "dlg/kitchen-staff"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-kitchen",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Two hatches. That is the entire distance between this fire and the duke's table, and it is why the kitchen is where it is. The fireplace is five and a half metres across — you could roast an ox in it, and on a feast day they did."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/kitchen-oven",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "How many cooked?",
                "next": "dlg/kitchen-staff"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/kitchen"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-kitchen",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Bread. You light a fire inside it, let it burn down, rake it out and put the loaves in on the hot stone. One firing does bread, then pastry, then whatever is left in the pot, in that order, as it cools."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/kitchen-staff",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "What is the oven?",
                "next": "dlg/kitchen-oven"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/kitchen"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-kitchen",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "A cook, and whoever the cook could shout at. Spit boys, a baker on baking days, and a man whose whole job was the fire. The drain in the floor tells you the rest of it — this room was washed down, not swept."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/royal-apartments",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Why up here?",
                "next": "dlg/royal-warm"
              },
              {
                "label": "Whose chair?",
                "next": "dlg/royal-chair"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-royal-apartments",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Guests slept over the kitchen, because the kitchen is the warmest wall in any castle. An audience chamber and two bedchambers, kept ready. Mary, Queen of Scots stayed at Doune more than once; so did her son's court, when the hunting was worth the ride."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/royal-warm",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Whose chair?",
                "next": "dlg/royal-chair"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/royal-apartments"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-royal-apartments",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Heat goes up. The kitchen fire burns from before dawn to after dark, and every stone of that chimney is on the other side of this wall. The best-lodged guest in a castle is always the one nearest the cooking."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/royal-chair",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Why up here?",
                "next": "dlg/royal-warm"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/royal-apartments"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-royal-apartments",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Lead on."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Whoever outranked everyone else in the room that week. The cloth over it matters more than the chair — you were not being shown a seat, you were being shown that somebody was entitled to sit while you stood."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/wall-walk",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Which films?",
                "next": "dlg/walk-films"
              },
              {
                "label": "And after?",
                "next": "dlg/walk-after"
              },
              {
                "label": "Is that everything?",
                "next": "dlg/walk-end"
              }
            ],
            "text": "That is the Teith, and those are the Highlands behind it. The walk goes the whole way round: garrison up here, everybody else down there. And you have seen this castle before, whether you know it or not — film crews have stood where you are standing."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/walk-films",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "And after?",
                "next": "dlg/walk-after"
              },
              {
                "label": "Is that everything?",
                "next": "dlg/walk-end"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/wall-walk"
              }
            ],
            "text": "A comedy about knights who have no horses. A series where this courtyard was a place called Winterfell. And a time-travel romance that brought more visitors than either. It is the coconuts people shout at me about."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/walk-after",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Which films?",
                "next": "dlg/walk-films"
              },
              {
                "label": "Is that everything?",
                "next": "dlg/walk-end"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/wall-walk"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Crown, then earls who mostly lived elsewhere, then the roofs in 1883, then the state took it on in 1984 and has kept it standing since. Six hundred years of nobody quite needing it and nobody quite letting it fall."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/walk-end",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Thank you.",
                "next": "dlg/muster"
              }
            ],
            "text": "That is everything that was built. I always finish here because of what is not: the fourth range, along that south wall, drawn and never begun. Six hundred years of a courtyard with one side missing, and nobody has ever had to explain why."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/muster",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Who are they?",
                "next": "dlg/muster-who"
              },
              {
                "label": "Why on foot?",
                "next": "dlg/muster-foot"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-wall-walk",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "We'll watch."
              }
            ],
            "text": "One thing more before you go down. The castle still turns its guard out at the end of the day, the way it did when there was a garrison over that gate. Stay where you are and watch the courtyard. They come out under the arch."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/muster-who",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Why on foot?",
                "next": "dlg/muster-foot"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/muster"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-wall-walk",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "We'll watch."
              }
            ],
            "text": "The duke's men. Twenty or thirty of them lived over the gate passage and ate in the hall you walked through: a captain, his men-at-arms, and a porter who never got a day off. The captain comes forward to the well. The rest cross and go in by the cellar door, which is how they always went to their dinner."
          },
          {
            "id": "dlg/muster-foot",
            "options": [
              {
                "label": "Who are they?",
                "next": "dlg/muster-who"
              },
              {
                "label": "Back.",
                "next": "dlg/muster"
              },
              {
                "effects": [
                  {
                    "objective": "obj/hear-wall-walk",
                    "type": "complete-objective"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "We'll watch."
              }
            ],
            "text": "Because the horses were stabled outside the walls and always were. A courtyard is for crossing, not for riding about in. And you have already heard what this castle is best known for, so you know the drill: no horses. If you want the sound of them you will have to make it yourself."
          }
        ],
        "npc": "npc/guide",
        "root": "dlg/approach"
      }
    ]
  },
  "dsl_version": "0.25.0",
  "stage": "dialogue"
}
